    # password_reset_confirmation_overlong_tenant_id_with_overlong_token
    # before appending; the existing confirmation locks cover
    # the no-record 406, the invalid/wrong-user/wrong-type/
    # expired token 406s, the weak new password 406, the
    # success 200, the missing-token and missing-user_id
    # 422s, the missing-body 422, the missing new_password
    # 422, the non-string new_password 422, the non-object-
    # body 422 (a JSON array), the scalar-body 422 (a JSON
    # string), the numeric-body 422 (a JSON number), the
    # boolean-body 422 (a JSON boolean), the null-body 422
    # (an explicit JSON null), the malformed-JSON 422, the
    # extra-field-ignored 406, the empty-token 406, the
    # whitespace-token 406, the empty-user_id 406, the
    # whitespace-user_id 406, the malformed-user_id 406, the
    # nonexistent-user_id 406, the overlong-token 406, the
    # overlong-user_id 406, the overlong-tenant_id 406, the
    # overlong-tenant_id-with-malformed-user_id 406, the
    # overlong-tenant_id-with-empty-user_id 406, the
    # overlong-tenant_id-with-whitespace-user_id 406, and
    # the overlong-tenant_id-with-nonexistent-user_id 406,
    # not an overlong tenant_id combined with an overlong
    # token. Verified fresh from account_endpoint.py:
    # password_reset_confirm takes token: str = Query(...)
    # with no max_length, user_id: str = Query(...) with no
    # min_length and no ObjectId type, and tenant_id:
    # str | None = Query(None) with no max_length, so all
    # three query values pass FastAPI request validation -
    # unlike the missing-token 422, where the param is
    # omitted - and the handler never passes tenant_id to
    # change_password (auth_service.py) at all - it is only
    # logged via _safe_log_value, which merely flattens
    # carriage returns and newlines and imposes no length
    # limit - so an overlong tenant_id cannot change the
    # outcome. The handler strips the token and the user_id,
    # both unchanged (neither carries surrounding
    # whitespace), and change_password first calls
    # retrieve_password_reset_data_by_user_id, where
    # PydanticObjectId("000000000000000000000000") parses
    # cleanly, so the broad malformed-id catch in
    # user_service.py is NOT taken and the repository lookup
    # single_or_none(user_id=object_id) runs instead; on a
    # fresh test DB no reset document carries the all-zero
    # id, so it returns None, and change_password raises
    # InvalidOperationException ("Invalid or expired
    # password reset token.") BEFORE any JWT verification,
    # password update, or notification email - so the
    # overlong token is never verified and the test depends
    # on neither the JWT secret nor any seeded record. The
